mod std.c;

/**
 * CALLING C: the names of C's types, and where a C library is
 *
 *      import ( std.c )
 *
 *      extern "c" {                                    // "c": the functions every C program has
 *          def strlen(text: string) : c_size;
 *          def abs(value: c_int) : c_int;
 *      }
 *
 *      extern "sdl" {                                  // a library of your own choosing
 *          def SDL_GetTicks() : c_uint;
 *          def SDL_Delay(ms: c_uint);
 *      }
 *
 *      def main() {
 *          c_library("sdl", "SDL2");                   // which file "sdl" is (not needed when the name is the file's)
 *          println("" + strlen("hello"));              // 5
 *      }
 *
 * AN EXTERN BLOCK lists functions of a C library, each without a body and
 * with the C function's own name. Nothing else is written - no C++, no glue:
 * the first call opens the library, finds the function and hands the
 * arguments over the way C wants them.
 *
 * THE TYPES below are the C types by their C names. Most are other names for
 * Sharp's own numbers; five are more than that, because C tells apart what
 * Sharp does not:
 *
 *      c_float                 a decimal in Sharp, a `float` (32 bits) in C
 *      c_long, c_ulong         C's `long`: 32 bits on Windows and on a 32-bit machine, 64 on 64-bit Linux
 *      c_size, c_ssize         C's `size_t` and `ssize_t`: as wide as an address
 *      pointer                 an address, as a number. 0 is C's NULL.
 *
 * and beside the numbers an extern function takes
 *
 *      string                  handed to C as a C string (a copy with the 0 at its end; null is NULL).
 *                              A C string that comes BACK is copied into a new Sharp string.
 *      _int8[], _int32[], decimal[] ...
 *                              an array of numbers: C gets the address of its items and reads and
 *                              writes the array itself. Tell C how many there are - it cannot know.
 *
 * WHERE A LIBRARY IS. The name in `extern "name"` is tried as a file: beside
 * the program, in the project's folder, in the folder the program was started
 * in, then wherever the system looks for libraries - with the system's ending
 * (.dll, .so) and `lib` in front added when they are left off. When the file
 * is called something else, say so before the first call with c_library(), or
 * in the project's settings.json ("c_libraries").
 *
 * A LIBRARY ONLY ONE SYSTEM HAS says so in front of its name:
 * `extern "windows:kernel32" { }`, `extern "unix:c" { }` (windows, linux,
 * mac, or unix for the last two). Its functions are left alone on the other
 * systems; ask build.is_windows() before calling one.
 *
 * WHEN SOMETHING IS WRONG. A library or a function that is not there is an
 * unsatisfied_link_error that lists what was looked for. C itself checks
 * nothing: a declaration that does not match the C function, or an address C
 * may not use, crashes the program - the crash report then names the C
 * function, how it was declared and what it was handed.
 *
 *      Java comparison: java.lang.foreign (the linker, ValueLayout.JAVA_INT ...);
 *      C#: [DllImport]; Python: ctypes.
 */

alias _int8   as c_char;
alias _uint8  as c_uchar;
alias _int16  as c_short;
alias _uint16 as c_ushort;
alias _int32  as c_int;
alias _uint32 as c_uint;
alias _int64  as c_longlong;
alias _uint64 as c_ulonglong;
alias decimal as c_double;

// (the compiler knows these six by name: backend/finalizer/sni/extern_functions.sharp)
alias decimal as c_float;
alias _int64  as c_long;
alias _uint64 as c_ulong;
alias _uint64 as c_size;
alias _int64  as c_ssize;
alias _int64  as pointer;

/**
 * Say which file the C library `name` is: a path (absolute, or looked for
 * beside the program, in the project and in the folder the program was
 * started in) or a file's name the system finds itself. The ending (.dll,
 * .so) may be left off. Said more than once, each place is tried in order.
 *
 *      c_library("sdl", build.is_windows() ? "SDL2.dll" : "libSDL2-2.0.so.0");
 *
 * Throws illegal_state_exception when the library is already open: say it
 * before the first call of one of its functions.
 */
def c_library(name: string, place: string) {
    library_name : _int8[] = name.data!!;
    library_place : _int8[] = place.data!!;
    asm {
        pushl [library_name]
        pushl [library_place]
        int 0x56
    };
}

/**
 * Open every C library the program's extern functions name and find every
 * one of those functions NOW, instead of at each one's first call - so a
 * missing library or a misspelt function shows when the program starts.
 * Throws unsatisfied_link_error with everything that is missing.
 */
def c_check_libraries() {
    missing : _int8[];
    asm {
        int 0x57
        popl [missing]
    };
    if(sizeof(missing) > 0) {
        throw new unsatisfied_link_error("$missing");
    }
}

/**
 * C WILL CALL THIS FUNCTION LATER - after the call it is handed to has
 * returned (an event handler, a timer, a comparison C stores).
 *
 *      on_tick := { interval: c_uint, data: pointer -> println("tick"); return interval; };
 *      c_keep(on_tick);
 *      SDL_AddTimer(1000, on_tick, 0);
 *      ...
 *      c_drop(on_tick);                    // C no longer has it
 *
 * A function handed to an extern function is C's for the length of that call
 * and no longer: enough for qsort and "call this for each". c_keep says C
 * keeps it; the function then has ONE address in C until c_drop, however
 * often it is handed over. Keep it BEFORE it is handed over.
 *
 * A callback runs on the thread that made the C call, while a C call of that
 * thread is running. For a C library that calls from threads of its own, see
 * c_serve_other_threads() (std/c/threads.sharp).
 */
def c_keep(callback: object) {
    asm {
        pushl [callback]
        int 0x58
    };
}

/** C no longer has the function (see c_keep). */
def c_drop(callback: object) {
    asm {
        pushl [callback]
        int 0x59
    };
}
